### Front line

Both Ukrainian monitoring project DeepStateMap and independent tracker UkrDailyUpdate reported no territorial changes for the day, recording zero square kilometers newly occupied, newly contested, or regained by Ukraine. 

In contrast, the Russian Ministry of Defence claimed its forces had cleared and captured Myrivka along with over 10 square kilometers of surrounding positions, and published statements recounting operations to take Dobropillya and Pervomaiske in Donetsk Oblast. None of these territorial claims were corroborated by the independent mapping sources. Additionally, *The Economist* war-fire model detected seven war-related satellite fires in Luhansk Oblast based on its estimates.

### Strikes and losses

According to geolocated imagery reviewed by the open-source monitor GeoConfirmed, a reported Ukrainian Storm Shadow missile strike caused visible damage to the Proton Factory outside Ukraine. The Russian Ministry of Defence claimed that Russian air defenses intercepted 530 Ukrainian fixed-wing drones throughout the day—443 overnight and 87 during daytime hours—across numerous Russian regions, the Black Sea, and the Sea of Azov.

The Russian Ministry of Defence also claimed widespread strikes across Ukraine, which remain unverified by other sources. These alleged targets included:
* Port infrastructure, uncrewed boat storage, and operator locations in Vylkove (Odesa Oblast);
* A dry cargo vessel bound for Odesa carrying military supplies, struck at sea;
* A rail car depot in Poltava and two locomotives near Kharkiv;
* A logistics center in Chernihiv;
* A Ukrainian Mi-24 helicopter on an aircraft apron;
* Norwegian-supplied NASAMS launchers near Shyroke and Bashmachka, and a P-18 radar near Kotlyarivka, reportedly struck by Russian drones;
* Positions targeted by glide bombs in Volnyanka and Krynytsi, and Ukrainian drone control posts struck by Grad rocket artillery in Sumy Oblast.

Regarding verified equipment losses, independent monitor WarSpotting documented one Russian Ural-4320 transport truck destroyed by a loitering munition near Hranitne in Donetsk Oblast. The Ukrainian Telegram channel *lost_warinua* published archival imagery of a Russian Ural-4320 fuel truck destroyed in March 2022 in Chernihiv Oblast.

### Official statements

Both sides confirmed a major prisoner exchange involving 270 individuals from each side. The Ukrainian Ministry of Defence stated that 270 Ukrainians—including National Guard, Azov fighters, border guards, other military branches, and nine civilians—were returned, many of whom had been held in Russian custody since 2022. The Russian Ministry of Defence reported that 270 Russian service members were returned in exchange and transferred to Belarus for medical and psychological assistance.

The Ukrainian Ministry of Defence reported holding talks with Norway's defense minister regarding urgent supplies of interceptor missiles, MANPADS, and the integration of additional missiles into NASAMS air defense systems. Ukraine's MoD also announced the expedited arrival from Denmark of more than 50,000 rounds of 20-mm ammunition for F-16 cannon systems, which are being used to counter jet-powered attack drones. Domestically, the Ukrainian government introduced updated mobilization exemption rules, establishing a 95% deferral quota for defense industry workers.

The Chief of the General Staff of the Russian Armed Forces announced that Russian troops would introduce a local ceasefire on the Kherson axis near Oleshky starting at 04:00 Moscow time on October 27 to allow for a humanitarian operation. Separately, the Russian MoD claimed that a Russian reconnaissance drone observed Ukrainian soldiers shooting a wounded comrade; this claim was unverified.
